 discounts and website plans.[2][5][10][13][15]\n\n---\n\n## A) Focus@Will (focusatwill.com)\n\n### 1. What happened to the company over time? Any shutdown, “death,” acquisition, or 2026 relaunch?\n\n**Launch and early history**\n\n- Focus@Will is described as a *neuroscience‑based music service* founded by musician and entrepreneur **Will Henshall**, co‑founded with John Vitale and Graham Lyus.[16][17]  \n- Press coverage in 2013–2014 (ABC News, TechCrunch, TechHive, Wired) presents it as an active startup offering a web and app‑based subscription service that uses specially curated instrumental music to improve focus and extend attention span.[16][18][26][29]  \n- Early articles state the company was backed by the Pritzker Foundation, other private investors, and Singularity University.[17][25]\n\n**Continuous operation vs. shutdown/acquisition**\n\n- The App Store listing for “Focus@Will: Control Your ADD” shows © 2025 Focus@Will Labs, Inc., implying the corporate entity was still active and maintaining apps at least into 2025.[30]  \n- Focus@Will’s own website in 2026 advertises the service, referring to **“16 years of longitudinal behavioral data across millions of completed work sessions”**, which suggests a continuous operation rather than a complete shutdown and restart.[20] Even allowing for some stretching in the “16 years” phrasing for marketing, the reference is to long‑term operation, not a fresh relaunch from zero.[20]  \n- The service has a live support site and help center (Zendesk‑based), with recent update timestamps in 2025, indicating ongoing customer support rather than a defunct product.[19]\n\nThere is **no evidence in press coverage or the company’s own materials of a shutdown, “death,” or acquisition around 2021 or any other year**:\n\n- Major tech press (TechCrunch, PCMag‑style writeups, NextBigFuture, ABC News, Wired) discuss funding and scientific claims but **do not report an acquisition by a larger company**.[16][18][21][25][26][29]  \n- There is no 2021 M&A coverage tying Focus@Will to any acquirer; the “Micro Focus” acquisition by OpenText in 2023 is unrelated and only appears because of similar naming.[27]  \n- The current Focus@Will site does not describe any 2026 relaunch, rebranding, or “starting again from zero”; instead, it presents continuity of data and users.[20]\n\nGiven the available information, the most supportable conclusion is:\n\n- Focus@Will **has operated continuously** since launch (with typical product iteration and research projects), and  \n- There is **no documented acquisition** and **no credible evidence** of a 2026 “from zero” relaunch.\n\n### 2. Neuroscience/science claims and research (peer‑reviewed vs internal)\n\n**Marketing claims**\n\nFocus@Will consistently markets itself as built on neuroscience:\n\n- It describes itself as “neuroscience‑based music service” and “new music service based on human neuroscience helping you maintain your productivity.”[16][24][25]  \n- ABC News reports Henshall claiming that the app engages the **limbic system** and that users increased focus time by about **400%** (20 minutes to 80–100 minutes).[26]  \n- TechHive notes that Henshall says playlists “make users more productive and increase attention span by 400 percent with long‑term use.”[16]  \n- NextBigFuture and related materials cite EEG studies showing an **11–12% increase in beta and theta frequencies** at certain brain regions, and self‑reported **200–400% increases in focus time** based on surveys of ~22,000 active users.[21][25]\n\n**Research type: internal vs peer‑reviewed**\n\nEvidence points strongly to **internal or collaborator “white paper” research and pilot studies**, not to independent, peer‑reviewed trials specifically validating Focus@Will:\n\n- TechHive reports that Henshall and a UCLA psychology team conducted “two years of in‑depth productivity research” with 200 alpha participants, but does not reference peer‑reviewed publications; the science primer cites general cognitive neuroscience rather than a detailed protocol.[16]  \n- A Wired critique of Focus@Will’s “neuroscience‑based” marketing notes that the company’s “science” page lists literature references but **provides no direct trial references for the app itself**, and that members of the advisory board (e.g., Dr. Evian Gordon, Dr. Stephen Sideroff, Dr. Ned Hallowell) lack published research directly supporting Focus@Will’s specific claims.[18][25]  \n- Focus@Will and NextBigFuture refer to EEG studies and behavioral trials but in the form of **internal reports and marketing claims**, not peer‑reviewed journal articles.[21][25]  \n- A “research white paper” titled *“The Influence of Streamlined Music on Cognition and Mood”* by Science Director Dr. Julia Mossbridge is highlighted by Focus@Will in an interview with The Medical Futurist; this is described as a **white paper** and positioned as the company’s key evidence, again not clearly documented as peer‑reviewed in a journal.[23]  \n- Focus@Will hosts a PDF “white paper on music & reading research” on its own site, which appears to be an internal study write‑up rather than an external, peer‑reviewed publication.[22]\n\nIndependent commentary and skepticism:\n\n- TechHive quotes neuroscientists who say they are unconvinced and note that **they are not aware of peer‑reviewed studies demonstrating that a specific music program can “lock in and take over your attention span”** as Focus@Will claims.[16]  \n- Wired similarly criticizes the absence of peer‑reviewed evidence for Focus@Will’s specific productivity claims, emphasizing the gap between general neuroscience references and app‑specific trials.[18]\n\nAdditional external evaluation:\n\n- The Institute of Noetic Sciences (IONS) blog describes a study in which participants using Focus@Will during vocabulary learning showed **significantly better performance on certain measures** compared with controls, after excluding the top 5% performers.[28] That write‑up appears on IONS’ site, not as a mainstream journal article specifically branded as a Focus@Will clinical trial. It shows **some positive effect in a structured experiment**, but again does not change the overall pattern: the main focus‑app validations are **small, collaborator‑run or internal studies and white papers**, not multiple independent peer‑reviewed clinical studies.\n\n**Summary for Focus@Will’s science:**\n\n- The company **heavily markets neuroscience claims** (limbic system targeting, EEG biomarker changes, 200–400% focus increases).[21][25][26]  \n- It relies on **internal research**, collaborator white papers (e.g., Mossbridge), and cognitive experiments described on company‑affiliated channels.[22][23][28]  \n- Independent reporting and critiques explicitly point out a **lack of peer‑reviewed, app‑specific studies** verifying the claimed 400% productivity boost.[16][18]\n\n---\n\n## B) Endel (sound wellness app)\n\n### 1) Evidence of billing/subscription complaints dominating negative reviews\n\nYour question cites a claim that ~58% of **critical** Endel reviews are about billing/cancellation/subscription issues. While I do not have access to the underlying quantitative analysis behind that exact figure, there is **clear, public evidence that billing and subscription anger are a major, recurring theme in negative user feedback**:\n\n**Trustpilot and aggregate review analysis**\n\n- Trustpilot’s Endel page features multiple reviews where users report **being charged ~$120 annual fees after what they believed was a free trial or after attempting to cancel**, calling it a “Total scam on billing!”[1][3]  \n- A third‑party 2026 review and user‑experience article notes that “several users on Trustpilot and the Apple App Store said they thought they were starting a trial but were charged the full annual amount the moment they tapped through to the next screen,” and explicitly states that **“the billing flow is the single biggest complaint”** and that cancellation is “harder than it should be.”[6] That article synthesizes many user reports and treats billing complaints as the dominant pattern.  \n\n**App store review patterns and independent scrapes**\n\n- App review aggregation sites (such as appshunter‑style scrapes for Endel’s iOS app) show critical reviews stating “woke up this morning and saw that it had already charged me the full price” and that users are requesting refunds after unintended annual charges.[11]  \n- A German app‑review analysis page specifically titled “Endel: Schlaf und Achtsamkeit Negative Bewertungen: Worüber sich …” summarizes negative review themes and states that **users frequently complain about unexpected charges and difficulty canceling subscriptions**, noting “billing issues” as one of the common themes alongside app crashes and missing features.[14]  \n\n**Reddit and community threads**\n\n- A detailed Reddit post in r/ProductivityApps labelled “Endel App Scam” describes users being **hit with charges exceeding $100 after a 7‑day trial, with no straightforward cancellation path** and poor support response.[4] Other commenters explain that cancellation is possible through Google Play but highlight confusion around the flow.[4]  \n- A follow‑up Reddit thread addressing the “scam” post acknowledges that “over 80% of the users are expressing similar frustrations about this situation,” referring to recent reviews complaining about billing and refunds.[7] While that “80%” figure is anecdotal, it again points to billing grievances as a major share of negative sentiment.  \n\n**App Store responses from Endel**\n\n- In Apple App Store review replies, Endel’s team repeatedly clarifies that **billing and refunds are controlled by Apple/Google**, not by Endel directly, suggesting they are responding to frequent billing complaints and trying to steer users to App Store subscription management.[9][12]\n\nTaken together, these sources support:\n\n- Billing/charges and cancellation friction are **prominent and recurring subjects** in negative Endel reviews across Trustpilot, App Store comments, and Reddit.[1][3][4][6][11][14]  \n- Independent reviewers explicitly identify billing as **“the single biggest complaint”** and state that many users feel “tricked” into full annual subscriptions.[6]  \n\nWhile I cannot independently verify the precise **58%** figure for negative reviews without the underlying dataset, the available evidence is consistent with the claim that a **large share of critical reviews are about billing and subscription issues**, rather than just UX or sound quality.\n\n### 2) Company facts and engine architecture\n\n**Location and general profile**\n\n- Endel is widely described as a **Berlin‑based** company in tech press and on its own materials, positioning itself as a sound wellness and focus app delivering adaptive ambient soundscapes for focus, sleep, relaxation, and movement.[2][10][13]  \n\n**Engine: stems + rules vs neural‑net generation**\n\nPublic technical descriptions, engineering interviews, and community explanations consistently describe Endel’s system as:\n\n- A **real‑time generative engine** that uses pre‑recorded musical **stems** (instrumental layers) combined with **rules and contextual inputs** (time of day, weather, movement, heart rate, etc.) to algorithmically assemble soundscapes.[12]  \n- The engine is often framed as *algorithmic* rather than fully neural‑network/AI‑composed music. A 2026 Reddit post responding to “scam” claims says Endel “actually isn’t AI generated,” explaining that its engine uses **pre‑authored stems** combined with rules and context rather than end‑to‑end neural‑net “AI music.”[7]  \n- Endel’s support and app descriptions state that, once the user grants permissions, the app uses **timezone, weather, motion, and heart rate** to shape the soundscape.[12] This aligns with a rule‑based, parameterized generative approach.\n\nSo the best‑supported understanding is:\n\n- Endel’s engine is **rules‑based and context‑driven**, using stems plus logic and sensor/environment data, not a “black box” neural‑net audio synthesizer. It is sometimes marketed under “AI” or “adaptive” buzzwords, but technically it is **an algorithmic soundscape generator built on stems**.\n\n**Web/desktop version and pre‑rendered audio**\n\n- Endel’s App Store and website copy emphasize that the mobile apps generate soundscapes in real time; independent technical write‑ups and reviews note that the **desktop and web versions frequently stream pre‑rendered audio files** corresponding to specific soundscapes (e.g., Focus, Sleep, Relax), with playlists mirroring what the engine can produce.[2]  \n- The pattern described is:  \n  - The **core engine** builds adaptive streams on mobile and can render them into tracks.  \n  - The **web/desktop experience** often exposes those as pre‑rendered audio files/streams for convenience and compatibility, rather than running a full real‑time engine in the browser for every user.  \n\nThis aligns with the general architecture described in tech interviews and reviews: a central generative engine plus a catalogue of pre‑rendered content for platforms that benefit from simpler streaming.[2]\n\n### 3) Current US pricing (2026)\n\nYou asked specifically about the claim that Endel’s US pricing shows an annual list price of **$119.99/year** with heavy discounting down to about **$34.99**, plus a **lifetime** option at **$124.99**.\n\nThe best available 2026 sources show:\n\n**Apple App Store (US)**\n\n- Data from App Store tracking tools and the App Store listing for “Endel: Focus & Sleep Sounds” show:  \n  - **12‑month/Annual subscription: $119.99**.[5][15]  \n  - **1‑month subscription: $19.99** (one listing) and other monthly tiers from **$2.99 to $19.99** depending on offer.[2][5][15]  \n  - A **Lifetime** one‑time purchase option at **$124.99**.[5][15]  \n- SensorTower‑style analytics explicitly list “12 Months Annual $119.99” and “Lifetime One‑Time $124.99,” confirming those price points as valid SKU entries in the US App Store.[5]\n\n**Website and effective pricing / discounts (2026)**\n\nIndependent reviews and pricing trackers emphasise that **effective pricing for most users is lower than the App Store list price**, due to:\n\n- A 2026 Endel app review states:  \n  - “The US App Store lists monthly tiers from $2.99 to $19.99 and yearly tiers from $34.99 to $119.99, while Endel's own site runs rotating promos.”  \n  - It further notes that most 2026 independent reviews quote **standard pricing around $6.99–$9.99 per month or $49.99–$59.99 per year.**[2]  \n- A 2026 pricing comparison site summarises Endel’s App Store presence as:  \n  - **Monthly: $7.49–$19.99** (depending on offer)  \n  - **Annual: $59.99–$119.99** (depending on offer)  \n  - **Lifetime: $124.99** (App Store)[13]  \n- Another detailed 2026 review of Endel’s website‑based pricing states:  \n  - Website **monthly plan ≈ $5.99/month**  \n  - Website **annual plan ≈ $49.99/year**  \n  - Website **lifetime plan ≈ $89.99 one‑time**, with caveats about needing to let subscriptions expire before activating.[10]  \n- A separate country‑by‑country pricing tracker confirms that Endel’s subscription billed yearly is significantly cheaper via website in many regions than via the US App Store, consistent with these ranges.[8]\n\nPutting these together:\n\n- **Official US App Store list SKUs**:  \n  - Annual: **$119.99**  \n  - Lifetime: **$124.99**  \n- **Real‑world effective prices for many US users in 2026** (through website offers, promos, and non‑maximal SKU tiers):  \n  - Annual: **≈ $34.99–$59.99** (lower‑tier App Store SKUs plus website discounts)[2][10][13]  \n  - Monthly: **≈ $5.99–$9.99** (website and typical app offers)[2][10][13]  \n  - Lifetime: **≈ $89.99** via website, versus **$124.99** in the App Store.[10][13]\n\nSo your claim is broadly consistent with public information:\n\n- The App Store **does list an annual SKU at $119.99 and a lifetime SKU at $124.99**.[5][15]  \n- Independent 2026 reviews and pricing aggregators confirm **lower annual tiers down to ~$34.99 and common effective annual prices around $49.99–$59.99**, thanks to discounted SKUs and website promos.[2][10][13]\n\n---\n\nIf you’d like, I can break this down further into a compact comparison table (Focus@Will vs Endel) focused on business continuity, scientific validation, and pricing structures.","refusal":null,"reasoning":null,"annotations":[{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://www.trustpilot.com/review/endel.io","title":"Read Customer Service Reviews of endel.io","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://www.neurobeatx.com/blog/endel-app-review-2026","title":"Endel App Review (2026): Pricing, Pros, Cons & Alternatives","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://www.trustpilot.com/review/endel.io?page=3","title":"endel.io Reviews 48 - Trustpilot","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://www.reddit.com/r/ProductivityApps/comments/1qslbk9/endel_app_scam/","title":"Endel App Scam : r/ProductivityApps","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://app.sensortower.com/overview/1346247457?country=US","title":"Endel: Focus & Sleep Sounds - Overview - Apple App Store","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://nubiapage.com/endel-review-2026-login-download-app-cost-user-experience-and-faqs/","title":"Endel Review 2026: Login, Download, App, Cost, User Experience ...","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://www.reddit.com/r/ProductivityApps/comments/1uz9m4l/addressing_the_recent_scam_post_and_explaining/","title":"Addressing the recent scam post and explaining how Endel actually isn’t AI generated","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https: